# Jupyter-deploy: Terraform AWS EC2 JupyterLab template

## Project organization

This project manages the AWS resources to deploy a single-user JupyterLab application on a remote
Amazon EC2 instance, reached from your laptop through a local proxy over a pinned-TLS connection and
authenticated with your AWS identity. **AWS credentials are the only prerequisite**.

It uses Terraform as the infrastructure-as-code engine and places the EC2 instance in the default VPC
of the AWS account. There is **no public URL and no Elastic IP** — the instance's public IP is resolved
live at connection time, and the client proxy pins the instance's certificate rather than its address.

Within the EC2 instance, it leverages `docker-compose` to run docker services:
- the `jupyter` service itself (JupyterLab, on `:8888`)
- a `traefik` sidecar to control ingress and TLS termination (serving a self-signed certificate on `:443`)
- an `auth-sidecar` service that validates the AWS-identity token via Traefik ForwardAuth
  [protocol](https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/reference/routing-configuration/http/middlewares/forwardauth/)
- a `fluent-bit` service for log forwarding and a `logrotator` service for log rotation

The instance only allows ingress on port 443 (HTTPS), which it routes to the `traefik` service. The
security group opens `:443` to `0.0.0.0/0`; the real security boundary is the pinned self-signed TLS
plus the short-lived AWS-identity token that `jd proxy connect-info` mints — not the network layer.

Most `jupyter-deploy` commands act on the host via the `ssm-agent`.
Refer to [AWS SSM](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/session-manager.html) for more details.
SSM is used for host/server administration only — **not** for the JupyterLab data path.

The interactive `jd host connect` and `jd server connect` commands open an SSM session and therefore
require the [AWS Session Manager plugin](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/session-manager-working-with-install-plugin.html)
installed locally. It is **not** needed for `jd up`, `jd open`, `jd proxy`, or the `jd server logs`/`exec` commands.

The instance generates a long-lived self-signed TLS certificate at first boot and persists the key on the
EBS data volume, so it survives instance stop/start. The public certificate PEM is published to an AWS SSM
parameter, which `jd proxy connect-info` reads to pin the connection.

