### Manage access

{{ proxy-aws-sts-based-access }}

The **local caller** needs these IAM permissions for the proxy data path:

- `ec2:DescribeInstances` — resolve the instance's current public IP (there is no Elastic IP).
- `ssm:GetParameter` — read the published self-signed cert PEM (the TLS pin) from SSM Parameter Store.

Minting the token is a local presign that makes no API call, so it needs no IAM permission. The
security group opens `:443` to `0.0.0.0/0`, so no `ec2:*SecurityGroupIngress` permissions are required.
`jd host start`/`stop` additionally use `ec2:StartInstances` / `ec2:StopInstances` (`jd open` never
starts a stopped instance — it only launches the local proxy and opens a browser tab).

{{ iam-roles-users-for-proxy-cmd-instructions }}

The deploying identity is always authorized. There are two ways to change the allowlist:

- **`jd users` / `jd teams` (above)** — recreate only the `auth-sidecar` (~1-2s; JupyterLab keeps
  running) and write the change back into `iam_user_names_allowlist` / `iam_role_names_allowlist`.
- **Edit those variables and run `jd up`** — reconciles the running allowlist from the variables, but
  restarts the whole app (all containers). Prefer the CLI commands above for routine access changes.

The variables are the source of truth. Because the CLI commands write back to them, a later `jd up`
re-applies the same list (a no-op) rather than reverting a runtime change.

### Coding agents (Claude Code, Codex) on Amazon Bedrock

The jupyter image ships `claude` and `codex`, configured to call Amazon Bedrock. Bedrock access is
off by default: set `bedrock_enabled: true` in `overrides:` of `variables.yaml`, then run `jd config`
and `jd up`. The instance role then gets a policy limited to Bedrock inference, and the jupyter
container reads its credentials from the instance metadata service. The variables:
- `bedrock_enabled` attaches the Bedrock policy to the instance role
- `bedrock_model_patterns` lists the models the role may invoke (IAM wildcards)
- `bedrock_inference_scope` is `geo` (requests stay in the region's geography) or `global`;
  Codex has no geographic profile outside the US, so it needs `global` there
- `claude_code_version` and `codex_version` pin the CLIs in the image; each CLI's default model
  follows its version (Codex's is the one its release recommends for the inference profile prefix,
  set at image build time)

Verify from the jupyter container, headless (no first-run dialogs):

```bash
jd server exec -- "claude -p 'Reply with OK'"
jd server exec -- "codex exec --skip-git-repo-check 'Reply with OK'"
```

IMPORTANT: in an account that never used a model, the first invocation enables it, through an AWS
Marketplace subscription that the instance role cannot start, and accepts the model provider's
EULA. Anthropic models also need a one-time first-time-use form with the deployer's company details.
The deployer must enable each model in Bedrock for the account and region, for example by sending
one prompt from the Bedrock console playground. These are the deployer's decisions: never invoke a
model, accept an agreement or submit the form on their behalf.

Troubleshooting:
- **AccessDeniedException on every model, or Codex reports no model**: `bedrock_enabled` is
  `false`. Set it to `true`, then run `jd config` and `jd up`.
- **AccessDeniedException naming a model or inference profile**: the model is outside
  `bedrock_model_patterns`, or its inference profile is outside `bedrock_inference_scope` (Codex
  outside the US needs `global`).
- **Codex's model reads `unset: ...`**: the deployment gives Codex no model to use; the placeholder
  in `/etc/codex/config.toml` names the variable to change.
- **AccessDeniedException on a model the patterns allow**: the model was never enabled in the
  account and region; see above.

